#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150} :activity/id #uuid "15f10ca2-71ff-5122-8f23-034d870b20ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049796 :topic/id #uuid "1478960d-9b90-58d8-9424-023723660815" :topic/math-academy-id 2034} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130432 :tutorial/content "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5941 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37af3d14-d0da-5833-8cc2-8d34a27c4558" :step/math-academy-id 36260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129054 :question/id #uuid "d6839658-bf90-55e5-845c-c3ded22c7735" :question/math-academy-id "e-9672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129452}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" :step/math-academy-id 36261 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129055 :question/id #uuid "bed9f966-a72e-56d4-acb9-3e156d763c96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233424}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" :step/math-academy-id 36263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130433 :tutorial/content "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5942 :tutorial/title "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" :step/math-academy-id 36266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130437 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5998 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" :step/math-academy-id 36625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130438 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5999 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" :step/math-academy-id 36626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152}}] :activity/title "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129373}]}
